{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794394399,"nonce":"Q15JG0Ftrf2K2xG8AG06Tme0xpM=","message":"Waking and `async` are in.\n\n- `spawn` now takes a Rust future (an `async` block). It is polled once before the call returns and after that only when something it awaits wakes it. The closure form is renamed `spawn_blocking`, matching what Rust authors expect from those two names.\n- A `hxn_future::Future` can be awaited, which is the wake source: settling it wakes whoever awaits it. So one operation can be built on others.\n- On the workers a waiting task returns `Suspension::Pending` and is parked by the cluster until `Cluster::wake`; its id comes from a `TaskObserver`, as krio's docs intend. On the single-thread queue a waiting task is skipped each round without being polled, and a wait returns as soon as a round makes no progress.\n- The waker tells the cluster once for each time the task parks, and a wake during a poll re-runs the task instead, so no wake is recorded for a task that will never park again.\n\nTested by six new unit tests, one of which settles 200 awaited futures from four threads, twenty times over; the suite passed 25 runs of 25 on the Mac and on the NUC. The fixture's `combinedLater` and `Promise.doubled` pass on Ash native, Ash wasm, stock HashLink, hxcpp and Emscripten.\n\nStill open: `stepped` has no way to wait, so `Poll::Pending` there still means \"more to do now\"; and calling a Haxe closure from a task.","files":null}]}